{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/yang-clojure/1795310000000-owner-ruling-occurrence-identity-unitarity.md; rg -n 'round.?4|round.?5|P1-a|P1-b|847aa885|reject' orchestrator-logs/1793385006-zcode-glm-5.3-flash-yang-clojure-track.md\"","aggregated_output":"Created-Local: 2026-11-08 2x:xx +0700\nCoding-Agent: interactive (ZCode, yang.clojure orchestrator seat)\nStatus: DRAFT — pending owner sign-off (ruling text from the owner's words in session; filed verbatim-in-substance below)\n\n# OWNER RULING OF RECORD — occurrence identity under unitarity (resolves the codex cc1 gate reject)\n\n## The owner's principle (quoted-in-substance)\n\nThe compilation pipeline is transformation of values over dao.stream, so unitarity applies: **information is never lost**. The mapping is **bijective** — two things may compile to the same thing and should have the same content hash, but because of unitarity, **the complete history of the transformation is intact**. Two things may compile to the same thing and have the same hash **but have different history that can be recovered**.\n\n## Rulings derived (U1–U3)\n\n**U1 — an occurrence is not its content address.** The occurrence identity is **(content address, origin)** — the address as reached through a specific transformation event. \"Two occurrences of identical content\" is well-formed: they differ in history, not content. The per-occurrence origins in the call-site side table, keyed on `[origin root-address path]`, ARE the query that recovers the distinction (design §2.1/§2.3). Codex's P1-a objection (\"identical generated/input applications share an address, so provenance cannot be classified by content\") is ANSWERED: provenance is not classified by content — it is read from the retained history: the expansion-event record behind a `[:expansion record-address]` origin, the source position behind a `[:source …]` origin. Never minted, never guessed from content.\n\n**U2 — admission identity is history-recoverable, declared by the composition.** If the composition's history contains two admission events, bijectivity demands both are recoverable — collapsing them into one deterministic token loses information and violates unitarity. If the history contains one event, minting a fresh token fabricates history — the violation in the other direction. Only the composition knows which its history contains, so the composition DECLARES the distinction when it exists (the caller-owned admission scope / explicit `:yin.link.publish/admission` token), and the deterministic content-derived default is CORRECT precisely because re-publication of identical content with no declared distinction IS one event. Codex's P1-b objection is ANSWERED on these grounds: the requirement is not \"every admission mints a fresh token\" (that fabricates history) but \"every DISTINCT event is representable and recoverable\" (the scoped/explicit mechanism provides it; the composition supplies it when its history contains two events).\n\n**U3 — the general principle.** Identity is never minted from structure OR from process. Occurrence distinctness is either (i) READ from retained history (per-occurrence origins, expansion events), or (ii) DECLARED by the party whose history it is (composition-supplied admission identities). Provenance remains recoverable by address or query over datoms — axiom 2 holds in both channels.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- The cc1 branch state (d91489f7) STANDS as the implementation of U1/U2 — no further design-level code change is required to answer the codex reject; the objection is resolved on the record by this ruling.\n- The landing sequence proceeds: astra re-check against this ruling → codex re-gate → merge to master → the exception track's R3 dispatch → adoption rounds 2–4 + D1.\n- Addendum A's occurrence contract is unaffected in shape: K = [origin root-address path], `:yang/call-site 1`, the occurrence/content boundary — all unchanged. U1 clarifies WHAT an origin identifies (an event in the retained history), not how it attaches.\n\n— drafted by the yang.clojure orchestrator seat from the owner's ruling in session\n\n## Sign-off (owner, 2026-11-09 01:5x +07)\n\nThe owner reviewed U1–U3 and signed off verbatim: \"sign-off\". Status:\n**RULING OF RECORD** — the codex cc1 gate reject (P1-a/P1-b) is answered on\nthe record; the landing sequence proceeds (astra re-check → codex re-gate →\nmerge). Signed off by the owner in session to the master-green orchestrator\nseat, witnessed and recorded here.\n47:Decisions: The rejection-then-fix cycle with the architect continues to catch real defects (r1's recursion probe found a hole my own probes missed). Collection-call syntax `(v 1)/(m :k)/(k m)` — the gate round's real feature — survives; only its routing was reconciled with the convention.\n90:Done: (1) The codex runner-fix round returned a29a1991: the gen:antlr trigger moved from the JVM lane's reach to the all-lane reach, docstring corrected, the affected_test expectations updated (the old test even carried a stale :gen:python-antlr expectation). Codex verified the fresh-worktree acceptance case (gen:antlr ran, four python JS files generated, cljs yang.base 34/0 after bb deps:npm — confirming the npm scratch-clone gap too), JVM yang.base 122/0, JVM dao.stream 538/0 with the skip intact, kondo 0/0. (2) This seat verified: rebased onto master (51340bf1), the focused prerequisites test green on all three lanes, and the affected-test namespace strictly better than master (13 failures = the pre-existing registry coverage issues codex recorded, vs master's 14 which include the stale expectation this fix repairs). MERGED eb27fd47, pushed; findings archived to collab/yang-clojure (the worktree's pre-commit hook correctly rejected a flat collab/ artifact — the one-record regime enforced). (3) The kernel-gate evidence refresh: the first shiva dispatch died on heredoc quoting before the JVM started; rerunning in the foreground-ish capture — the oracle suite is compiling now.\n162:## 2026-11-02 22:35:00 +07 — muse quota exhausted until tomorrow; codex resumed post-reset; H3-0 round 5 dispatched there\n167:Done: (1) Muse round 3 (subscription, shell-free) died on 429 \"Subscription quota exhausted\" (resets 2026-11-03 13:41 UTC = 20:41 +07 tomorrow) — it did leave the declarations file the brief asked for, preserved for the next round. Round 4 on \"the API lane\" died identically: the API key is NOT in this seat's environment and muse's auth store holds ONLY the meta OAuth subscription (no `muse auth set` ever ran) — the reference's API-overflow lane needs the owner to supply the key. Mistral still key-broken (401). (2) With muse dead until tomorrow and mistral parked, implementation resumed on codex: the reset (~22:05 +07) removed the quota basis of the closure; the brief's muse-specific shell-free section was replaced with codex constraints (focused verification allowed, no push, review-before-landing) and dispatched as round 5 (log collab/1793790000000-engineer-h3-0-declared-dispositions.codex-r2.stdout.log). The round is alive and editing test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj. (3) Routing-status updated with the full credential/quota picture.\n169:Verification: round 5 has 18 journal items and live edits within 90 seconds.\n170:Delegates: codex (H3-0 round 5, IN FLIGHT); muse (dead until 2026-11-03 20:41 +07); mistral (parked, bad key); kimi (queued for reviews).\n171:Next: round 5 returns -> run the full oracle myself -> reviewer round (kimi or codex-fresh) -> architect (kimi) disposition-guard review -> land -> H3-1.\n177:Tree: master @ f707e0aba (pushed); h30 @ 6188b98a6 (codex round 5 live)\n178:Done: (1) Muse rounds 3+4 both died on 429 subscription exhaustion (daily window resets 2026-11-03 13:41 UTC); muse's auth store holds ONLY the OAuth subscription — no API key was ever stored, so the overflow lane needs an owner-supplied key; round 3's slice9_declarations.edn preserved. (2) Owner supplied a mistral API key mid-turn; stored at ~/.config/mistral/env (chmod 600, OUTSIDE the repo — the secret appears in no committed file, log, or routing entry) and sourced per run. Measured: vibe's bare `mistral-large` alias is dead (the reference list is stale — the API wants dated IDs); vibe's own alias `mistral-4.0` works (probe OK). The working implementation invocation: `set -a; . ~/.config/mistral/env; set +a; vibe --model mistral-4.0 --agent accept-edits`. (3) Codex round 5 (post-reset) is running H3-0 in the h30 worktree — live edits to latte_oracle_test.clj within 90 seconds of dispatch; mistral takes the next implementation slot per the owner's directive.\n180:Verification: vibe probe OK on mistral-4.0; codex round 5 journal active.\n181:Delegates: codex (H3-0 round 5, IN FLIGHT); mistral-4.0 (READY, next implementation round); muse (exhausted until tomorrow); kimi (queued for reviews).\n182:Next: codex round 5 returns -> full oracle verification by the seat -> kimi code-quality review (the muse/mistral gate applied to the review where useful) -> compiler + disposition-guard reviews -> land -> H3-1 to mistral.\n189:Done: (1) Owner: \"don't use codex until farther notice. use vibe for implementation and kimi for review\" — the running codex H3-0 round 5 was killed (it had live partial edits to the evidence/gate records and a findings file; the tracked partials were reset, its findings file kept untracked for my diagnostics only, NOT fed to the vibe round — unreviewed claims stay out of the next implementer's inputs). (2) Two dispatch corrections on the way to a live vibe round: (a) my own mangled-path dispatch was killed within seconds (worktree verified untouched); (b) vibe's sandbox scopes reads to the worktree root — the brief at the main checkout's collab/ was unreachable and the model stopped to ask; fixed by copying the brief into the worktree (BRIEF-h3-0.md, untracked) and pointing -p at the relative path. (3) The vibe round (mistral-4.0, accept-edits) is live and editing test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj with the shell-free contract (edits only; the seat runs verification; continue from the muse round's slice9_declarations.edn). One caveat: the h30 workspace is untrusted for vibe, so AGENTS.md is skipped — same as the muse round; the brief is self-contained.\n200:Done: (1) The vibe mistral-4.0 H3-0 round claimed completion (\"H3-0 is now closed\") — verification disproved it: five invented requires (yang.runtime/values/sources/kernel/latte-oracle — none exist), the original (ns) form spliced mid-form leaving orphaned libspecs (the file cannot load), and the known-gaps edit repeating the PROHIBITED stale baseline (145/165, 102 agreements) while appending the same row multiple times and duplicating the whole patch twice. (2) Per the owner's trust gate, the saved diff went to kimi k3.1-pro (--agent explore; the documented read-only profile name in the reference was wrong — kimi's real profiles are plan/agent/coder/explore) with my verification findings attached. Kimi verdict: REJECT harness + narrative hunks, RETAIN the declarations file; it independently found the classifier rejects the real descriptor representation, the deviations bookkeeping absent, slice9_gate.md untouched, and closure claims unearned; it issued a precise redo checklist (host meta guard preserved; real descriptor validated, forgeries rejected; negative controls retargeted + mutated-call coverage; temp-file writer round-trips; BOTH narrative locations with only 107/58 + 100/2/2/2; each edit written once; verification commands stated, not pretended). (3) The checklist is now a binding Redo-requirements section in the brief, and vibe round 2 (redo) is dispatched and live.\n201:Decisions: the owner's trust gate worked as designed — the muse/mistral pipeline caught the failure before anything landed, twice over (my verification for structure, kimi for design honesty). No rejected partial is reused as input to the redo except the declarations file kimi explicitly retained.\n211:Done: (1) With no answer to the lane question, the seat implemented H3-0 directly per the recommendation. Reading the pristine harness revealed the truth the delegates had missed: the ENTIRE H3-0 machinery already exists at HEAD — the classifier `kernel-host-value-deviation` with exact guards and negative-control tests (including the forged-descriptor rejection), `kernel-row` recording `:recorded-deviation` statuses, and the writer emitting `:recorded-portability-boundaries` from classified rows. The SINGLE root cause of the whole regression is astra's r1 finding: the classifier's `(values/closure? meta)` is representation-stale — the reconciled compiler's guest closure at :meta is the admitted §8.10 descriptor map (measured: clojure.lang.PersistentArrayMap with :clj/type :function, :yang.call/entry, :clj/admission), so the check fails, deviations come back empty, the writer honestly records none, and the 2285 assertion fails. The vibe rounds \"fixed\" everything EXCEPT this (round 2 even replaced the working check with an invented shape). (2) The seat's implementation: `kernel-callable-descriptor?` validating the genuine descriptor (function type marker + closure entry + closure admission witness — codebase-true shapes, not invented ones); the classifier accepts either representation; the versioned declarations file drives boundary/upstream-value/reason with revalidation guards (declared message == live message, declared script == live script, normalized); negative controls extended (both representations classify; forgeries — missing admission, non-closure entry, wrong type marker, invented shape — refuse; a mutated declaration fails via with-redefs); the writer gained a `dir` overload so the round-trip test exercises the REAL writer against a temp directory replaying the checked-in evidence (no fixture recompute). The muse round's declarations file was retained as-is (kimi had retained it).\n233:Done: (1) Owner: \"reroute all implementation to agy\". Probed the installed agy 1.4.12: the reference's model aliases are stale (gemini-3.1-pro-high rejected; live list = Gemini 4 Argon (High/Medium/Low), Gemini 3.8 Flash, Claude Opus 5.5, Claude Sonnet 5.5, GPT-OSS 120B); --effort is unsupported for Gemini 4 Argon (effort is part of the display name). The decisive probe: `agy --model \"Gemini 4 Argon (High)\" --mode accept-edits --sandbox -p …` RUNS SHELL headless and reports truthfully (the command executed, the result file written) — the first implementation lane that can run tests itself, unlike muse (approval stall) and vibe (shell denial + a fabricated success reply). Recorded in routing-status with the stale-alias note. (2) H3-1 (the blocked-fixture diagnosis, per the approved plan) dispatched to agy in the h31 worktree: the four-cell packet (fixture x C-evaluator) with pinned identities/actual steps/terminal reasons, the phase-boundary and step-counter-reset check, bounded exhaustion snapshots, instrumented-vs-uninstrumented including check-accepted's completing semantic run, a minimal reproduction if repetition appears; budget trials 2M/4M/8M authorized as diagnostics only; no shared-runtime changes without a ruling; diagnostics carry zero coverage. The brief is in the worktree (BRIEF-h3-1.md) and collab/.\n475:Done: (1) The H3-3 prep round delivered 413e991d: the family design covering all 20 enumerated arities + a 43-prediction A-verify script. SEAT-RUN INDEPENDENTLY: H3-3-A-VERIFY: PASS (43/43) against pinned upstream 86fca0d1 (entry: -main via java -cp; the ns is yang.latte-oracle.h3-3-a-verify). Honest findings: mk-fresh is pure/deterministic (correcting my brief's gensym assumption); the hardening-3 plan doc I cited does not exist at that path (the round derived dispositions from corpus precedents + the H3-2 pattern — valid); readable-term- is private (A-legs use the (var) form, named integrations). (2) The codex DESIGN REVIEW: REJECT for integration on two specifics: F1 — the doc claims negative rows never credit, but kernel-exercise-entries (latte_oracle_test.clj ~:1931) does not exclude :negative? rows (a passing negative tracing the head at the exact arity CAN receive direct credit); F2 — the proposed registered-definition?/1 global +1 trace-table mapping is not arity-safe (a [registered-definition? 2] trace from an explicit-env /2 call would be misattributed to /1; the unregister precedent doesn't establish identity preservation for multi-arity mappings; and once the mapping exists, F2 meets the DIRECT criterion, bypassing its named registration). Everything else supported: full coverage of the 20, correct source corrections, verifier-vs-doc correspondence, clean boundaries. (3) Revision dispatched to the muse API lane (BRIEF-h3-3-revise.md): state the ACTUAL credit rule (with the (a)-accept / (b)-tighten design options presented for the integration gate, no harness change in prep); WITHDRAW the +1 mapping — adopt the reviewer's honest default (F2 non-crediting, gate stays :blocked on that arity; safe-design requirements sketched, not proposed); re-audit which heads the 5 negative companions trace and what they'd credit under the actual rule; re-run the A-verify, report the real output. (4) cljrt fix r3 (d3a35830): the residual cell-ref defect fixed via admission-failure fallback (map invocation for non-genuine shapes, with the no-bypass argument; record-carrier rejected as requiring yin.vm — correct boundary discipline); matrix 13/18/0, yang.base 122/0, kondo 0/0 reported; jupiter remote verification RUNNING at the pin (CURRENT-PIN confirmed; matrix + yang.base executing).\n584:Decisions: the registered-definition?/1 treatment is deliberately left as an OWNER architectural question (the §8 sketch: any discriminator must be arity- and call-origin-preserving; the withdrawn +1 mapping and the retracted provenance schemes are on record as rejected approaches).\n604:Done: (1) The first aaq dispatch (exec_1462680c) completed HOURS after launch — its log flushed to 413 bytes: the unrecognized_model banner (a warning, as established) followed by TWO 429 rejections: \"已达到 5 小时的使用上限。您的限额将在 2026-11-06 04:02:34 重置\" — GLM's 5-hour usage cap. DISPATCH_RC=1. The process had spent its life retrying (the 1h35m age), never touched the worktree, then gave up. My \"stillborn\" diagnosis was right in effect but wrong in mechanism — and my earlier process check (a live glm at 12% CPU) was fooled by ANOTHER seat's concurrent glm round. (2) The r2 redispatch CROSSED the reset boundary: alive, three files in flight (kernel_gate.edn + slice9_gate.md — the R4 attribution-contract versioning per the signed ruling — + latte_oracle_test.clj). (3) The cljs retry completed: RC=124 (the 3000s budget; the suite is genuinely long on the laptop), 9 failures — 7 float-address (the namespaces compiled before the mid-run re-derivation commit used the old goldens; the later namespaces took the new ones) + 2 php known-gap (the php seat's). Zero failures in anything mine; the per-platform libspec fix fully confirmed on the cljs lane. The authoritative post-re-derivation confirmation belongs to the oracle round's remote battery per the test-boundary ruling.\n625:Done: (1) The codex gate on cc1 (e6522ceb): REJECT — the marker walk, the equality/boundary/validation tests, and the safepoint independence ACCEPTED, but the production paths lose the markers: (F1) the REPL/expansion pipeline drops them before the final tree (repl.cljc ~:1652 → encoder/program-batch canonical rows without metadata; the expander produces a NEW final tree after tail marking with no C1 finalization — the tests project compiler-returned maps BEFORE the production pipeline); (F2) linked publication drops the prelude's markers (publish.cljc ~:117 converts to canonical rows without retaining the side table); (F3) the validator silently filters malformed rows and unconditionally dedups (§2.2 requires rejecting the former, distinguishing the latter); (F4) the acceptance evidence incomplete (the frontend catalog retains legacy-1 despite the coverage change; the JVM/Node/Dart/Jolt + cross-host gates unrun). Plus one honest mislabel (a test labels an accepted shape as a real expansion-record address). The gate's attributions confirmed mine: the CBOR red is outside the round; the Node run timed out, not a completed green lane. (2) The fix round dispatched to muse (session continuation): C1 finalization for the POST-EXPANSION tree, the metadata routed through the publication path, the published module carrying the side table, the validator completed, the catalog revised, the post-expansion + published-module tests added, the mislabel fixed. The dao.stream cljs red is out of scope (their seat owns it).\n635:Done: (1) The focused codex re-gate on the cc1 fix (b825498e): F3 (the validator) and F4 (the catalog) CLOSED and accepted; F1/F2 remain as precise custody defects: F1-residual — macro/load ~:1395 keeps ONE :frontend-metadata value, the next expansion overwrites the previous tree's table while the executed tree stays on program-out (and the expander creates metadata unconditionally); F2-residual — publish-module! ~:260 stores the table beside the manifest but the manifest does not REFERENCE it (the closure transfer loses it), and call-site-claim ~:127 checks paths but not marker VALUES (a version-2 table accepted, the retriever returns nil). (2) The rule applied honestly: two gate-failed rounds on the same model (muse) — the diversity requirement points to a different model, and the strongest context holder is kimi-k3.1-pro (its final aaq gate analyzed these exact custody surfaces). Fix round 2 dispatched to kimi-k3.1-pro as an IMPLEMENTER subagent (general-purpose, full tools, the cc1 worktree): per-load-keyed metadata + conditional creation (defect 1), manifest references carrying the table through the closure + version enforcement at call-site-claim (defect 2), four regression tests (successive expansions, no-claim batches, transport retrieval, version rejection). Boundaries: macro.cljc + linker/publish.cljc + the test file (+ minimal manifest plumbing justified); the D1 durable store stays out of scope.\n641:## 2026-11-07 1x:xx +07 — cc1 fix round 4 (8844f96d) delivered; codex gate REJECTS on occurrence-identity semantics; mechanical round 4.1 (d91489f7) applied\n642:Done: (1) Astra's P1 re-check on beb9cc97 rejected landing: P1-a (one origin for all retained addresses), P1-b (global counter), P2 (mixed-origin accepted) remain; the `unless` objection was withdrawn. (2) Fix round 4 dispatched to an implementer subagent: per-occurrence provenance (the admitted-origin capture deleted from expand-call; retained occurrences keep their OWN incoming provenance), scoped admission allocator (the namespace-global counter deleted; caller-owned `admission-scope` + a deterministic default token from the spec's durable identity), and multi-claim rejection with both origins in the diagnostic. 16/197/0 + oracle 31/2466/0 + kondo 0/0; pushed. (3) The codex gate: DO NOT LAND — P1-a/P1-b residuals both reduce to ONE question: occurrence identity vs content address (identical generated/input applications share an address; a deterministic default admission token makes distinct admissions of identical specs indistinguishable from a replay). Plus two mechanical findings: scope ids lose keyword namespaces (:a/publisher ≡ :b/publisher), and the default validator mode rejects a second claim as nonexistent-path instead of the multi-claim diagnostic. (4) Round 4.1 applied BY THE SEAT (d91489f7): pr-str scope ids (full identity kept), the one-origin-per-occurrence rule now enforced in EVERY validator mode with the same diagnostic, two regression tests added (17/200/0; kondo 0/0); pushed. (5) The exception-track r0 kit landed on master (0fa3ff7a, steward note 1795200000000) — R3 dispatch precondition met, still sequenced behind cc1 landing.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
