on tests (successive expansions, no-claim batches, transport retrieval, version rejection). Boundaries: macro.cljc + linker/publish.cljc + the test file (+ minimal manifest plumbing justified); the D1 durable store stays out of scope.\nDecisions: the fix round goes to a different model per the diversity requirement — kimi implements, codex re-gates (the reviewer/implementer roles stay separate).\nVerification: the gate's file/line refs are the spec; the four regression tests are the acceptance.\nDelegates: kimi-k3.1-pro (cc1 fix round 2 implementer, IN FLIGHT); codex (the next re-gate, queued).\nNext: kimi's fix lands -> my verification -> the focused codex re-gate -> ACCEPT -> merge -> adoption rounds 2-4 + D1 -> the generalization completes.\n\n## 2026-11-07 1x:xx +07 — cc1 fix round 4 (8844f96d) delivered; codex gate REJECTS on occurrence-identity semantics; mechanical round 4.1 (d91489f7) applied\nDone: (1) Astra's P1 re-check on beb9cc97 rejected landing: P1-a (one origin for all retained addresses), P1-b (global counter), P2 (mixed-origin accepted) remain; the `unless` objection was withdrawn. (2) Fix round 4 dispatched to an implementer subagent: per-occurrence provenance (the admitted-origin capture deleted from expand-call; retained occurrences keep their OWN incoming provenance), scoped admission allocator (the namespace-global counter deleted; caller-owned `admission-scope` + a deterministic default token from the spec's durable identity), and multi-claim rejection with both origins in the diagnostic. 16/197/0 + oracle 31/2466/0 + kondo 0/0; pushed. (3) The codex gate: DO NOT LAND — P1-a/P1-b residuals both reduce to ONE question: occurrence identity vs content address (identical generated/input applications share an address; a deterministic default admission token makes distinct admissions of identical specs indistinguishable from a replay). Plus two mechanical findings: scope ids lose keyword namespaces (:a/publisher ≡ :b/publisher), and the default validator mode rejects a second claim as nonexistent-path instead of the multi-claim diagnostic. (4) Round 4.1 applied BY THE SEAT (d91489f7): pr-str scope ids (full identity kept), the one-origin-per-occurrence rule now enforced in EVERY validator mode with the same diagnostic, two regression tests added (17/200/0; kondo 0/0); pushed. (5) The exception-track r0 kit landed on master (0fa3ff7a, steward note 1795200000000) — R3 dispatch precondition met, still sequenced behind cc1 landing.\nDecision pending THE OWNER: the occurrence-identity ruling — per-occurrence provenance for identical subtrees requires either (a) composition-supplied durable admission identities (the composition asserts which admissions are distinct events), or (b) admission-minted occurrence identity (admission-time token distinguishes otherwise-identical occurrences). In a content-addressed store identical content IS one address (unitarity) — whether \"two occurrences of identical content\" is meaningful, and who decides distinctness, is the owner's call. My recommendation: (a) — it keeps the unitarity axiom (identity never minted from structure) and puts distinctness where the knowledge is (the composition).\nDelegates: oracle full run (background); shiva remote battery at d91489f7 (background).\nNext: both green + the owner's allocator ruling → the design-level fix → astra re-check → codex gate → land → R3 dispatch → adoption rounds 2-4 + D1.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
